WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.263
Think about a list you already use:

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.970
contacts, places to visit, or resources

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:09.890
for a community activity. We will turn
a fictional resource list into a small

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.222
browser app that helps
someone find an entry.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:13.730
Your experience

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:14.650
with lists gives

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.381
you a useful starting point for
deciding whether the result makes sense.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.095
You do not need to become a
professional programmer today.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.255
You will practice directing a build,

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:27.670
checking what it does,
and making one controlled improvement.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.230
The first version has one page,

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:34.630
a search field, a category filter,
and readable results.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.170
Keep the records fictional so

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:41.315
the work can be shared and tested without
exposing anyone's private information.

00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:45.613
A website can present
information for people to read.

00:00:46.107 --> 00:00:49.707
A web app responds to input,
such as filtering our resource list.

00:00:50.587 --> 00:00:51.787
Software as a service

00:00:51.787 --> 00:00:55.507
usually provides an ongoing hosted
service and may include accounts,

00:00:56.187 --> 00:00:59.247
stored data, subscriptions,
and operational support.

00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:02.699
Our one-file practice
app is not all of that.

00:01:03.207 --> 00:01:04.287
It has no real account

00:01:04.287 --> 00:01:09.167
system, private database, or payment
service. This distinction helps you ask

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:13.327
for an achievable first version.
A convincing sign-in screen does

00:01:13.327 --> 00:01:16.107
not prove secure authentication
exists behind it.

00:01:16.947 --> 00:01:20.937
Start by describing the useful behavior
you can actually test in the browser,

00:01:21.387 --> 00:01:22.279
then treat hosting,

00:01:22.527 --> 00:01:27.167
security, and service operations as
separate decisions that require more work.

00:01:28.976 --> 00:01:31.336
HTML describes the content and structure:

00:01:32.056 --> 00:01:35.336
a heading, a labeled search field,
and a list of resources.

00:01:36.456 --> 00:01:39.596
CSS controls how those parts
are arranged and displayed.

00:01:40.716 --> 00:01:45.416
JavaScript responds to input, such as
deciding which records match a search.

00:01:46.516 --> 00:01:48.896
Think of a familiar form: the questions

00:01:48.896 --> 00:01:49.816
and fields are

00:01:49.816 --> 00:01:51.116
its structure, their

00:01:51.116 --> 00:01:51.996
presentation helps

00:01:51.996 --> 00:01:55.716
you read them, and the rules determine
what happens when you use it.

00:01:55.716 --> 00:02:00.316
The analogy is a starting point,
not a complete description of software.

00:02:01.176 --> 00:02:02.056
Open the supplied

00:02:02.056 --> 00:02:04.436
starter in the browser
and identify a visible

00:02:04.436 --> 00:02:07.976
example of each part.
Changing a heading is different

00:02:07.976 --> 00:02:10.308
from changing the rule
that filters the list.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.070
Define success in terms
someone else can observe.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:20.690
First, searching for library should show
the fictional Community library entry.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:26.930
Second, searching for an unmatched term
should show a clear no-results message.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.901
Third, the controls should
work with the keyboard.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.947
Add a check for different letter
cases and a narrow screen.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.430
These are acceptance checks:

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:41.808
statements about behavior that help you
decide whether the result is usable.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:45.350
Avoid a requirement
such as make it amazing,

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.190
because it does not explain what to test.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:53.450
Pause and write three checks for
a small app idea of your own.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:55.350
Ask a partner

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:59.090
whether they could carry out each
check without guessing what you meant.

00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:02.879
A useful prompt describes the user,

00:03:03.439 --> 00:03:07.159
the task, the controls,
the data boundaries, and the checks.

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:08.989
For example:

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:10.279
build one local

00:03:10.279 --> 00:03:14.519
HTML page that searches fictional
community resources by name and category.

00:03:15.479 --> 00:03:16.559
Include labeled fields,

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:21.439
visible keyboard focus, a no-results
message, and no external dependencies.

00:03:22.779 --> 00:03:26.251
Keep all example records fictional
and explain how to run the file.

00:03:27.139 --> 00:03:31.159
Ask the AI to describe the important
parts so you can inspect its work.

00:03:32.119 --> 00:03:36.539
Do not put passwords or private API keys
into the prompt or the browser code.

00:03:37.379 --> 00:03:40.021
People can inspect code
delivered to their browser.

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:42.579
A longer prompt is not
automatically better;

00:03:43.279 --> 00:03:46.906
each instruction should clarify
behavior or a boundary you can verify.

00:03:48.488 --> 00:03:51.234 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Use the instructor-approved
tool if one is available,

00:03:51.748 --> 00:03:53.561 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
or begin with the supplied starter.

00:03:54.788 --> 00:03:58.788 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Save the file with its HTML
extension and open it in the browser.

00:04:00.148 --> 00:04:04.448 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Confirm that the heading, search field,
filter, and results appear.

00:04:05.728 --> 00:04:08.788 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Keep this first working version
before asking for changes.

00:04:09.868 --> 00:04:11.908 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
If no AI account is available,

00:04:12.408 --> 00:04:15.569 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
you can still complete the
exercise with a plain-text editor:

00:04:16.148 --> 00:04:19.068 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
change the heading and
one fictional resource,

00:04:19.988 --> 00:04:22.448 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
save the file, and refresh the browser.

00:04:23.608 --> 00:04:27.468 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Be careful that the editor has not
silently added a text-file extension.

00:04:28.548 --> 00:04:31.088 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The goal is a working
result you can explain,

00:04:31.948 --> 00:04:35.160 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
not evidence that you used a
particular paid service or model.

00:04:36.826 --> 00:04:37.846 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Type library into

00:04:37.846 --> 00:04:41.628 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
the search field and compare the result
with your first acceptance check.

00:04:42.126 --> 00:04:48.306 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Then enter an unmatched term such as zzz.
The page should explain that there

00:04:48.306 --> 00:04:52.586 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
are no matching resources rather than
appearing broken or showing an old result.

00:04:53.646 --> 00:04:57.746 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Clear the field and try
LIBRARY in uppercase.

00:04:57.746 --> 00:05:00.566 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
If the intended behavior is
a case-insensitive search,

00:05:01.106 --> 00:05:02.561 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
it should find the same record.

00:05:03.066 --> 00:05:06.846 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Next combine the search with
a category filter and inspect

00:05:06.846 --> 00:05:12.246 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
whether both conditions apply. Record
expected and actual results separately.

00:05:13.466 --> 00:05:15.386 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A confident statement from the AI is

00:05:15.386 --> 00:05:18.866 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
not test evidence.
The evidence is what happened

00:05:18.866 --> 00:05:21.626 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
when you carried out a named
check on the saved file.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.923
Set the mouse aside and move through
the controls with the keyboard.

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:28.845
Can you see which control has focus?

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.723
Can you enter a search
and operate the filter?

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:35.673
Narrow the browser window and
check that labels, results,

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.343
and buttons remain readable without
important content being cut off.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.848
Then ask where the data lives.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.218
In our starter,
fictional records are inside the file.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.040
Browser storage, if

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.520
added later, may remain only in
that browser and may be cleared;

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.100
it is not automatically a
shared database or a backup.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.360
Test persistence rather than assuming it.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.000
Use the course's existing text-size

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.109
and accessibility habits as
standards for the app you build.

00:06:04.772 --> 00:06:06.652
Suppose the uppercase search failed.

00:06:08.272 --> 00:06:11.018
A useful revision request
names the observed problem,

00:06:11.252 --> 00:06:13.169
the expected behavior, and the check:

00:06:13.992 --> 00:06:15.532
LIBRARY returns no result,

00:06:16.092 --> 00:06:18.061
but library returns Community library;

00:06:19.052 --> 00:06:22.834
make both searches match the same
entry and preserve the category filter.

00:06:23.892 --> 00:06:26.327
Save the revised version
under a distinct name.

00:06:26.992 --> 00:06:30.515
Repeat the failed check, then repeat
a check that previously passed.

00:06:31.392 --> 00:06:35.693
This second step can reveal a regression,
where fixing one behavior breaks another.

00:06:37.252 --> 00:06:40.620
If the new version is worse,
return to the saved working version.

00:06:41.732 --> 00:06:44.332
Pause and make one small
improvement or repair.

00:06:45.332 --> 00:06:49.477
Keep a brief test log so a partner can
follow what changed and why you trust it.

00:06:51.113 --> 00:06:54.844
Show a partner the saved app and carry
out your three acceptance checks.

00:06:55.513 --> 00:07:00.333
Explain one part of the page, one change
you made, and one limitation that remains.

00:07:01.513 --> 00:07:03.213
A local fictional prototype can

00:07:03.213 --> 00:07:07.358
be a successful learning result without
being ready to operate a public service.

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:09.393
Hosting is a separate choice.

00:07:10.213 --> 00:07:11.923
A real service handling accounts,

00:07:12.153 --> 00:07:12.903
private data,

00:07:12.993 --> 00:07:15.153
or payments needs appropriate security,

00:07:15.733 --> 00:07:19.613
access controls, support,
and testing beyond this exercise.

00:07:20.793 --> 00:07:23.539
Do not treat a decorative
sign-in form as protection.

00:07:24.773 --> 00:07:28.093
Carry forward the method you practiced:
define a small task,

00:07:28.733 --> 00:07:29.860
give clear instructions,

00:07:30.473 --> 00:07:31.365
inspect the result,

00:07:31.693 --> 00:07:35.713
test more than the easiest path,
and keep a way back when you revise it.
